  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\pi ,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\pi ) & =\u27e84\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi \u27e9=\u27e8-4,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=\\pi $ we calculate the magnitude of $v(\\pi ),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(\\pi )| & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=\\pi $ is $4.$",
